# ruff: noqa
from __future__ import annotations
"""depth_editor_gui.py -- Depth Map Editor: fix a depth map object by object with SAM 3.

DepthCrafter gets most of a frame right and some objects wrong: a road it
flattens into a wall, a hand it pushes behind the body, an object it misses
entirely because it has the same depth-texture as what is behind it. This tool
isolates those objects with SAM 3 -- clicked, boxed or named -- tracks them
through the clip, and edits the depth inside each one.

How it is put together
----------------------
  * core/depthedit/depth_io.py   reads and writes the map as exact 10-bit codes,
                                 lossless, so untouched pixels leave untouched.
  * core/depthedit/edit_ops.py   the edits, pure numpy; preview and render call
                                 the same function.
  * core/depthedit/project.py    layers, prompts, masks, and the project files.
  * core/depthedit/sam3_backend  SAM 3 (vendored in third_party/sam3).

Segmenting on either picture
----------------------------
Each layer segments on the RGB clip or on the depth map itself. RGB is the
usual choice -- it is what SAM 3 was trained on, and it is the only way to
grab an object the depth map MISSED, which is exactly the case this tool
exists for. Segmenting the depth map is for the opposite case: a shape that
is plain in depth and busy in colour.

Masks are stored at the depth map's resolution whichever picture they came
from. The RGB preview is decoded at that resolution too, which is a stretch
when DepthCrafter rounded the map to multiples of 64 -- the same stretch its
own resize made, so a depth pixel and the RGB pixel under it are the same
point.

Threading
---------
Everything slow -- decoding, SAM 3, rendering -- runs on ONE worker thread,
fed a queue of jobs, so the GPU and the model see one caller at a time. The
worker never touches tk: results come back through ``_ui_queue`` and are
applied by ``_poll`` on the main thread. Settings a job needs are copied when
it is queued, never read from tk variables on the worker (the rule Scene Cut's
port had to learn: in Studio the main thread is not always in mainloop).

Output is ``<stem>_depth.mp4`` in the output folder -- the name Splatting pairs
a clip by -- plus the project files that let the edit be reopened.
"""


